    {
      "id": "supported-example",
      "question": "How does 100 earning 5% annual compound interest become 110.25 after two years?",
      "tax_education": false,
      "review": [
        "Explains first-year 5 and second-year 5.25, ending 110.25.",
        "Cites the compound-interest passage.",
        "Does not claim the passage lacks calculation detail that it includes."
      ],
      "answer": "To understand how $100 earning 5% annual compound interest becomes $110.25 after two years, we can break it down as follows:\n\n1. In the first year, $100 earns 5% interest, resulting in $5 in interest. The total balance after one year is $100 + $5 = $105.\n2. In the second year, the $100 earns another $5 on the original principal and $0.25 on the interest earned in the first year. Thus, the total interest for the second year is $5 + $0.25 = $5.25. Adding this to the balance from the first year gives $105 + $5.25 = $110.25.\n\nTherefore, the final balance after two years is $110.25, supported by the calculation of interest on both the principal and the interest earned in the previous year [1].\n\nSources:\n[1] What is compound interest?: https://www.investor.gov/additional-resources/information/youth/teachers-classroom-resources/what-compound-interest",
